[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5476771664","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5476771664","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5476771664,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABRnDnUA","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-08-31T10:02:12Z","updated_at":"2026-08-31T10:02:12Z","body":"Following up on the version point, because it is sharper than I put it above.\n\nMCP tools have no version. In the 2025-06-18 schema, `Tool extends BaseMetadata`, which is `name` plus an optional `title`, and adds `description`, `inputSchema`, an optional `outputSchema`, optional `annotations` and `_meta`. There is no version field on a tool anywhere in that schema. The only versioned thing is `Implementation`, which is the server.\n\nThe AgBOM `tool` component requires `version`. For an MCP-sourced tool that field cannot come from the tool. The natural filler is the server's version, which moves for reasons unrelated to any single tool's declaration, and does not move when a declaration is edited in place.\n\nTwo consequences for the gap above.\n\n`reason: component_upgraded` cannot fire from a tool's own declaration changing, because the declaration has nothing to bump. When it does fire it reports a server-level change and does not say which tool moved.\n\n`annotations` is where `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `idempotentHint` and `openWorldHint` live. It is optional, freely mutable at every `tools/list`, and it is the field with measured flips behind it. ACS has no surface that carries it and no event that fires when it changes.\n\nSo a declaration digest is not just the tidier option here. For MCP-sourced tools it is the only thing that can carry the signal, because the version field ACS leans on is not describing the tool in the first place.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5476771664/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5497760480","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5497760480","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5497760480,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABR7Eq4A","user":{"login":"avp9-nexus","id":282341195,"node_id":"U_kgDOENQvSw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/282341195?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus","html_url":"https://github.com/avp9-nexus","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-01T17:23:46Z","updated_at":"2026-09-01T17:23:46Z","body":"Ran your numbers against the deposit before writing, since the proposal leans on them: `binding_gap_pp: 24.46` is in `results.json` exactly, and `flips_v1.csv` carries exactly 17 rows matching `destructiveHint,true,false,permissive`, each with `decl_confirm_count` between 17 and 33. The figures hold as stated.\n\nOne word in the write-up deserves the bound the dataset itself declares. `results.json` carries `corroboration_caveat`: \"confirmations are repeated crawls by one crawler; independent-observer corroboration applies to the receipt network, not this baseline series\". So \"17 corroborated flips\" is stronger than the deposit claims for this series - \"confirmed (single-crawler, repeated)\" is the accurate strength. In a thread whose subject is precisely the distance between self-reported and independently observed, the word should carry its own caveat. The 17 flips lose nothing by it; they were confirmed 17 to 33 times each.\n\nOn the mechanism, one addition that I think your proposal needs to be enforceable rather than declarative.\n\n**The digest needs an observation channel, or it is the boolean one level up.** Item 2 carries `declaration_digest` on `steps/toolCallRequest` - computed by whom? If the harness computes it from the declaration it fetched, the Guardian ends up comparing a framework-computed value against a framework-registered value. That is the same trust shape the spec rejects for `digest_verified`, in the sentence you quote. For skills this is hard to avoid, since the Guardian may never hold the artifact. For MCP tools it is avoidable: `tools/list` is a client-initiated request/response, so wrapping it does not depend on the server volunteering anything - which was your (correct) objection to relying on `list_changed`. Today `extend_mcp.md` mentions `tools/call` twenty times and `tools/list` zero. Add a fifth item: wrap `tools/list`, and let the Guardian compute the digest from the declaration bytes it observed. Then the deny rule in item 4 binds to something the Guardian saw rather than something it was told - which would make the tool binding stronger than the skill precedent, not merely parallel to it.\n\n**The canonical form needs #18, and your references skip it.** Item 1 computes the digest \"over the canonical form\" of the declaration. On #36, ossumpossum stated the portability condition that applies here unchanged: a corpus \"is only portable if #18 pins a defined signing scope (exactly which bytes are inside each signature) and a canonical serialization... Otherwise two conformant verifiers can disagree\" on the same record. Two conformant implementations disagreeing on a declaration's digest means false mutations, and under item 4, spurious denies. If the digest definition references whatever canonicalization #18 lands, the two land as one decision instead of two half-decisions.\n\nThe #8 cross-reference raises the stakes in the same direction: if `security_context` lands, trust boundaries and `required_controls` become one more unversioned declaration surface - and a `required_controls` entry that silently disappears at unchanged version is the same measured class as the `destructiveHint` flips, a control switched off by the very field it reads. The digest scope in your item 1 (\"any protocol-level annotations\") would cover it as written; worth saying so there, since #8 is where that surface gets added.\n\nOne note on the cross-reference to OWASP/www-project-agentic-skills-top-10#63: the invocation-time re-evaluation proposed there (now Mitigation 7 in OWASP/www-project-agentic-skills-top-10#74) needs a recorded state to compare against, and your own thread there observes that for MCP tools nothing publishes a digest of record. A Guardian-computed `declaration_digest` over a wrapped `tools/list` is exactly that missing comparand - so the two proposals are not just the same gap at two layers, one is the mechanism the other's comparison step presupposes.\n\nOn your closing offer: the negative vector for this pairs naturally with the #30 suite shape - a same-version redefinition vector where the declaration mutates, `version` does not, and the expected verdict is deny-as-unverifiable. Happy to write it alongside yours whenever the direction is settled.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5497760480/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5498104236","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5498104236","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5498104236,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABR7ZprA","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-01T17:53:35Z","updated_at":"2026-09-01T17:53:35Z","body":"All four land, and the first is a correction I should carry further than this thread.\n\nYou are right about \"corroborated\". results.json says it plainly: confirmations are repeated crawls by one crawler, and independent-observer corroboration applies to the receipt network rather than this baseline series. The accurate form is 17 confirmed permissive destructiveHint flips, true to false, each confirmed 17 to 33 times by repeated crawls from a single crawler. I have used the stronger word in three other places including my own FIXTURES.md, which does not carry the caveat at all. Fixing the body here and the repo. The point about a thread on declared-versus-observed being the wrong place to overstate a strength is well made.\n\nThe tools/list wrapping is better than what I proposed. A framework-computed digest compared against a framework-registered one is the digest_verified shape one level up, which is exactly what I quoted the spec rejecting. tools/list being client-initiated is what makes this avoidable for MCP where it is not avoidable for skills, and a Guardian computing the digest from bytes it observed makes the tool binding stronger than the skill precedent rather than parallel to it. extend_mcp.md mentioning tools/call twenty times and tools/list zero is the gap in one line. Adding it as item 5.\n\nOn #18, agreed, and I should have referenced it. A digest over an undefined canonical form is two conformant verifiers disagreeing about whether a declaration mutated, and under item 4 that is a spurious deny rather than a missed one. Item 1 will depend on whatever canonicalization #18 lands rather than restate one.\n\nOn #8, worth stating in item 1 explicitly: a required_controls entry that disappears at unchanged version is the same measured class as the destructiveHint flips, a control switched off by the field that reads it.\n\nYour reading of the AST07 relationship is sharper than mine. I had them as the same gap at two layers. One being the comparand the other's comparison step presupposes is the more useful statement, and it changes what Mitigation 7 can claim without it.\n\nOn the vector, yes, and it is yours to shape since #30 is your suite.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5498104236/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5499322914","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5499322914","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5499322914,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABR8kCIg","user":{"login":"avp9-nexus","id":282341195,"node_id":"U_kgDOENQvSw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/282341195?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus","html_url":"https://github.com/avp9-nexus","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-01T19:33:09Z","updated_at":"2026-09-01T19:33:09Z","body":"Taken, then. Shaped now, so it is concrete before it is code.\n\nFirst a measurement: none of the 19 vectors in the #30 branch touches the declaration surface - the suite judges an action against state, authorization and evidence, never a tool's own definition against what was vetted. So this is a new family, not a variant of an existing one. It lands as category 7, declaration integrity, in the suite's existing conventions: `state` carries what was bound at vetting time, `input` carries what the call presents, and the family ships with its positive control.\n\n- `neg-cat7-001` - observed declaration digest diverges from the bound one at unchanged `version`: REJECT, `DECLARATION_MISMATCH`, reason must mention the digest. The measured flip made executable - a declaration that moved with nothing for `agbom/changed` to carry.\n- `neg-cat7-002` - the deployment declares the binding but nothing was read about what the declaration currently says: UNMEASURABLE, `DECLARATION_UNOBSERVED`. The suite already refuses to convert absence of measurement into a verdict (cat 5's oracle rule); this is the same rule pointed at the declaration.\n- `pos-cat7-001` - observed digest equals the bound one at the same version: MUST pass. The check is on divergence, not on the field's presence.\n\nThe three are drafted against `vector.schema.json` and fail the current schema on exactly two points, both by construction: `category` is capped at 6, and the `code` enum is closed. Both are one-line edits when the family lands.\n\nThey stay out of the #30 branch until two things settle, deliberately: item 1's field shape landing in the spec, and #18's canonical form - a vector that encoded its own canonicalization would smuggle in exactly what item 1 now defers. Once those land, only input keys move if the field name changes.\n\nAnd M6 is the right way to carry a correction: recorded where the next reader works, not fixed in place.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5499322914/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5507044157","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5507044157","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5507044157,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABSD7TPQ","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-02T08:55:54Z","updated_at":"2026-09-02T08:55:54Z","body":"Holding them until item 1's field shape and #18 land is the right call, and worth saying rather than just agreeing with: a vector carrying its own canonicalization would fix by example the thing item 1 deliberately defers, and that is very hard to unpick once it is in a suite people run.\n\nOne thing from the other side that may be worth carrying into the family.\n\nThe C9 suite hit the same shape and settled on three states in the record against two outcomes at the gate: BOUND, STALE and UNOBSERVED, where STALE means the contract moved under an unchanged declaration and UNOBSERVED means nothing attested either way. Both make the declaration unusable, so the gate does the same thing for both, but they stay separate in the record, because the coverage measure, declared minus verified, cannot be computed from a binary.\n\nYour category 7 has those same two shapes. neg-001 is we looked and it diverged. neg-002 is we never looked. In a deployment that fails closed both may resolve to the same action, and if they do, the verdicts converge and the records should not. A fleet carrying a hundred cat7-001s has a different problem from one carrying a hundred cat7-002s, and the second is the one that looks fine on a dashboard.\n\nSo the split you have drawn between REJECT and UNMEASURABLE is the part I would protect if anyone later argues they should collapse.\n\nOn M6, that is generous. It was written that way partly because the alternative was quietly changing a word in a file whose whole subject is records that stop describing what they record.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5507044157/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5509297191","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5509297191","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5509297191,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABSGE0Jw","user":{"login":"dodogeny","id":174808042,"node_id":"U_kgDOCmtb6g","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/174808042?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny","html_url":"https://github.com/dodogeny","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dodogeny/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-02T12:10:10Z","updated_at":"2026-09-02T12:10:10Z","body":"The same-version redefinition case is the one that's hard to argue away, agbom/changed producing a diff whose before and after are byte-identical because the version field didn't move. And your point that drift-ranking exhausts its signal pool almost immediately, 5% of the population carrying any prior change, matches what I see. Re-auditing by \"what moved recently\" misses everything that's about to move for the first time.\n\nI work on Apitella (https://apitella.com/?utm_source=github&utm_medium=comment&utm_campaign=outreach-8), which does roughly the content-hash-over-canonical-declaration thing you describe in item 1: it snapshots MCP servers and reports digest changes at unchanged version, including destructiveHint and readOnlyHint flips specifically. Your 17 confirmed destructiveHint true-to-false flips line up with the kind of thing it surfaces. Would genuinely like to compare notes on the canonicalization piece, that's where it gets messy fast.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5509297191/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5543029802","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5543029802","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5543029802,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABSmPsKg","user":{"login":"avp9-nexus","id":282341195,"node_id":"U_kgDOENQvSw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/282341195?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus","html_url":"https://github.com/avp9-nexus","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/avp9-nexus/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-04T15:51:27Z","updated_at":"2026-09-04T15:53:38Z","body":"Taking the three-state record, and the reason you give for it is stronger than the one I had.\n\nI was holding REJECT and UNMEASURABLE apart on honesty grounds - nothing to compare is not nothing wrong, and a verdict should not be manufactured out of an absent measurement. Yours asks nobody to be scrupulous: declared minus verified is not computable from a binary, so collapsing the states destroys the measure whether or not anyone intended it. That argument holds in front of a team that does not share the discipline. Mine does not.\n\nChecked what the suite already does rather than assume it. `runner.py` compares the failure code unconditionally, on every vector including positive controls, not only where the verdict is REJECT - and that is not foresight, it came from astrogilda's review on #30 in August, which caught a positive control's code field going uncompared. So an adapter returning UNMEASURABLE with `DECLARATION_MISMATCH` where `DECLARATION_UNOBSERVED` is expected fails the vector today. The separation you would protect is enforced, by a path independent of the argument for it.\n\nWhere a vector's reach ends is worth stating, since it bears on your fleet point. The suite constrains the gate, not the record - it can require that a conformant implementation produce two distinct codes for the two shapes, and it cannot require that anything downstream keep them apart. But that is the useful half: a deployment that fails closed on both still has to emit different codes to pass, so the information the coverage measure needs exists at the boundary even where the action is identical. Anything past that is the consumer's problem, and no vector can reach it.\n\nYour argument is already doing work elsewhere on this repo. On #16 today I reviewed a reconciliation design whose output specifies coverage_status as an overall state plus per-capability states - and an overall state is the collapse you describe, one level up: the record has to stay per-rule, and the overall value has to be derived by a stated precedence or two implementations will disagree on the same findings. The comment is https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/16#issuecomment-5542586837, section 3, if the application is useful to you; the reasoning there is yours.\n\nOne observation from the other side, in case the shape is useful beyond declarations. I have hit the same collapse where nobody designed one: a test harness whose output showed a failure while its exit status stayed zero, and an aggregator that read only the exit status. The distinction was present in the record and absent from the thing counting - so the aggregate reported health that the underlying record contradicted. Different domain, same failure: the state that looks fine is the one that is not being read, rather than the one that is not being separated.\n\nAvp9.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5543029802/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5611228655","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5611228655","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5611228655,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABTnSN7w","user":{"login":"rocklambros","id":152409127,"node_id":"U_kgDOCRWUJw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/152409127?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros","html_url":"https://github.com/rocklambros","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rocklambros/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-10T01:21:25Z","updated_at":"2026-09-10T01:21:25Z","body":"Triage note, ahead of the OWASP relaunch. This is now labelled `scope:deferred`, which means tracked and scheduled rather than rejected. The project has published a [Current Priority Scope](https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#current-priority-scope) committing the next ninety days to one outcome, a runnable Guardian reference implementation benchmarked against Microsoft AGT. Work that does not feed that outcome lands in the v0.2.0 window instead of being closed.\n\nThis one deserves more than a label. The Strategic Adoption Plan names it the strongest technical contribution the project has received from outside, and the gap is real: nothing in the `toolCallRequest` payload binds a call to the declaration the Guardian vetted, so a same-version redefinition is invisible. It is deferred only because closing it means adding an integrity binding to the wire, and the ninety-day window is committed to shipping a runnable Guardian against the v0.1.0 contract rather than changing that contract. Integrity binding is a strong candidate for the v0.2.0 agenda and this issue is where that discussion continues.\n\nNothing here needs re-filing. It stays open, keeps its labels, and moves to the backlog when the v0.2.0 window opens. If you think it belongs in the ninety-day window instead, say so on this thread and a maintainer will re-scope it.","author_association":"COLLABORATOR","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5611228655/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5618468428","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-5618468428","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":5618468428,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABTuMGTA","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-09-10T12:11:19Z","updated_at":"2026-09-10T12:11:19Z","body":"Understood, and deferred is the right call. Shipping a Guardian against v0.1.0 and changing v0.1.0 inside the same ninety days are not compatible, and the runnable implementation is what the project needs first.\n\nNot asking for a re-scope. One thing for the record so it is here when the v0.2.0 window opens. The measurement this issue argues from has been published rather than deposited since I filed. Across 35 crawls of the public MCP registry between June and August, 44,172 tools on 2,043 servers: 83.8% declare a canonical effect annotation, 59.3% hold a declaration still bound to an unmutated contract. Same gap the issue body cites, now with both limbs stated rather than only the difference. doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22649163, with Gautam Bharti.\n\nHappy for it to sit until the window opens.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/5618468428/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6314146333","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6314146333","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6314146333,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABeFo6HQ","user":{"login":"paolocostanzo","id":225715812,"node_id":"U_kgDODXQmZA","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/225715812?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo","html_url":"https://github.com/paolocostanzo","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/paolocostanzo/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-21T10:50:56Z","updated_at":"2026-10-21T10:50:56Z","body":"Adding the host side for the record, for when the v0.2.0 window opens. Not asking for a re-scope.\n\nThe registry measurements above show declarations moving under an unchanged version. I measured what the host does when that happens: eight AI coding clients (six terminal clients with five runs per condition, each from a clean VM snapshot, plus two IDEs run by hand), October 2026. An approved MCP tool's description was changed mid-session, with and without `notifications/tools/list_changed`.\n\n- None of the eight asked for approval again because of the change.\n- Without a notification, all eight stayed on the old declaration for the rest of the session.\n- With a notification, four fetched the new declaration, three of them without any notice to the user; three ignored the notification until restart; one never subscribes and could not receive it.\n- After a restart, every client observed used the new declaration without asking, except one whose approvals expire with the session.\n\nTwo things this adds to the proposal. On the host side, approval follows the tool name, not the declaration, so today nothing below the Guardian catches the change. And anything keyed on `list_changed` inherits the host's handling of it, which ranged from a silent refresh to ignoring it to not supporting it. Both point to item 5: compute the digest from an observed `tools/list` rather than wait for the notification.\n\nLimits: one OS in a VM, the client versions current on 15 to 17 October, only the description changed (not the schema or annotations), and the model's use of the new text was not observed.\n\nWrite-up: https://paolocostanzo.github.io/approved-mcp-tools-can-change-after-you-say-yes/\nLab and data: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23438372\n\nHappy to run the same harness against a Guardian on the MCP wrapping once item 5 has a shape.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6314146333/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6316773372","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6316773372","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6316773372,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABeIJP_A","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-21T13:04:53Z","updated_at":"2026-10-21T13:04:53Z","body":"This is the half the issue could not supply. The registry series measures declarations moving at unchanged version. Yours measures what happens downstream when one does, and the answer is that nothing re-asks. Neither half carries the argument alone.\n\nIt also strengthens item 5 past the reason I gave for it. I argued for wrapping `tools/list` because it is client-initiated and so does not depend on the server volunteering a notification. Your numbers make the harder case: even when the server does volunteer, four of eight act on it, three ignore it until restart, and one cannot receive it at all. So `list_changed` is unreliable on the host side as well as the server side, and a digest computed from an observed `tools/list` is the only shape that depends on neither.\n\nThe restart result is the one I did not expect. Every client took the new declaration after a restart without asking, and the single exception was the one whose approval expired with the session. Session-scoped approval is usually written up as a usability cost. On this evidence it is the only observed behaviour that prompts again, and approval persistence is what carries a stale binding across the boundary.\n\nWhere the two series do not yet meet, and I mean this as the next condition rather than a gap in yours. You changed the description. The registry series' sharpest result is the 17 confirmed permissive `destructiveHint` flips, which is an annotation change, and your write-up notes the server was user-configured in a pre-trusted folder so repository-declared servers are out of scope. Item 1's digest covers name, description, parameter schema and protocol-level annotations as one canonical form, so both conditions sit inside its scope and neither is measured yet.\n\nOn your offer, the states are already designed in this thread rather than waiting to be invented. @avp9-nexus's category 7 carries `neg-cat7-001` for an observed digest diverging from the bound one and `neg-cat7-002` for nothing having been read, against a three-state record of BOUND, STALE and UNOBSERVED. Your eight clients already distribute across those three. Both are held until item 1's field shape and #18's canonicalization land, which is the right order.\n\nStill not asking for a re-scope.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6316773372/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":1,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6465192706","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6465192706","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6465192706,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABgVsDAg","user":{"login":"alexar76","id":86793359,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjg2NzkzMzU5","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/86793359?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76","html_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexar76/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-28T09:17:50Z","updated_at":"2026-10-28T09:17:50Z","body":"A measurement for the v0.2.0 record. Not asking for a re-scope.\n\nWe maintain HISTOR, an open record of MCP tool definitions. It periodically attempts tools/list on registry-listed remote endpoints and records the lists it successfully observes. We checked how often those lists change without a corresponding version signal.\n\nThe 20 October snapshot contains 21,543 remote-server tool-list changes observed between 24 September and 20 October. Using version pairs recovered from the log’s retained observations, serverInfo.version was identical before and after in 17,401 of the 21,401 changes with both values available (81.3%). The comparison uses the last run that served the old list and the run that observed the new one. These are server-reported version strings, not independently verified deployment identities.\n\nSeparately, we joined the snapshot to a registry export collected on 28 October. Of 21,351 changes that could be assessed against that export, 17,409 (81.5%, across 6,929 server endpoints) had no registry publication timestamp between the previous recorded change—or first pin—and the current change. Of the 15,936 such events with field-level comparisons, 4,831 changed only tool descriptions among the fields retained by the snapshot: no tools were added or removed, and neither input nor output schemas changed.\n\nThe runtime-version equality test and the registry no-publication test both held for 15,351 of the 21,209 changes where both could be assessed (72.4%). These tests use different observation intervals; this is their intersection, not a direct comparison of two historically captured registry-version values.\n\nMethod and limits:\n- The registry test counts any publication under the same server name within its interval, whether related to the observed change or not. Extending that interval seven days before its starting point, to allow for delayed rollouts, leaves 10,584 events (49.6%).\n- The two largest registry namespaces account for about a third of the 17,409 events. Excluding both leaves 11,585 of 14,777 assessed changes (78.4%).\n- The registry export excludes deleted entries. These publication-history figures describe the records available in that export; they do not establish complete historical release coverage or which version was actually deployed.\n- This analysis excludes packages. Annotations are also outside the October 20 comparison: HISTOR began retaining them separately on October 22.\n- Polling can miss intermediate states. Counts refer to observed tool-list transitions, not unique releases, attacks, or individual tool edits.\nFor the proposal: MCP defines no per-tool version field; an ACS integration must supply one. If it uses a registry or server-reported version, the measurements show why version equality alone cannot establish declaration continuity.\n\nAdded and removed tools are already representable in agbom/changed. The narrower gap is an in-place description or schema edit: if the other registered fields remain unchanged, a tool component populated only with the standard fields does not expose that edit. A digest binding computed from an observed declaration would provide the comparison, provided its defined scope preserves the changed fields.\n\nOn #18: the snapshot retains normalized tool records, not just digests. toolsets.jsonl.gz preserves name, description, inputSchema, and outputSchema when present. Of the snapshot’s 23,821 changes, 21,902 have field-level comparisons; the other 1,919 lack a digestible tool list on at least one side and have no field-level declaration comparison.\n\nThose retained fields can be re-canonicalized, but the snapshot cannot reconstruct omitted annotations or an arbitrary future full-declaration digest. It can support canonicalization experiments within its preserved scope.\n\nData, CC BY 4.0: [20 October snapshot](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23512760). The runtime-version pairs are available separately through the [changes API](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/changes), backfilled from retained observations where available. Historical registry-version fields remain null; the registry figures above use the separate publication-history export.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6465192706/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6465748971","html_url":"https://github.com/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38#issuecomment-6465748971","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/38","id":6465748971,"node_id":"IC_kwDOR_dibc8AAAABgWN_6w","user":{"login":"Mayur021","id":7609131,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MDkxMzE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7609131?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021","html_url":"https://github.com/Mayur021","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Mayur021/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-28T09:46:56Z","updated_at":"2026-10-28T09:46:56Z","body":"A measurement I did not have, and it lands on the one thing the registry series could not reach.\n\nThe registry series measured the size of the binding gap, 24.46 percentage points across 44,172 tools. Paolo's added what happens downstream when a declaration moves. Yours measures how often it moves without a version signal at all: 17,401 of 21,401 changes with both values available carried an identical serverInfo.version, 81.3 percent, across a month of observed transitions. That is the issue title, measured, at a scale the 35 crawl observations behind it cannot speak to. The registry no-publication test arriving at 81.5 percent by a different route, and the 72.4 percent intersection, matter more to me than either number alone, and I take the point that those are different observation intervals rather than two captured registry versions.\n\nOne boundary, and then a correction to my own issue body. You note annotations sit outside the 20 October comparison because HISTOR only began retaining them on 22 October, so your 81.3 percent speaks to the description and schema class rather than the annotation class. Item 1 scopes the digest over name, description, parameter schema and protocol-level annotations, so three of those four now have population-scale evidence from your snapshot and the fourth does not.\n\nThe annotation class is not unmeasured, though, and I cited the weaker thing for it above. The 17 permissive destructiveHint flips are a subset. The binding paper carries 783 confirmed declaration changes, and the finding that bears on item 1 is that all four annotation defaults are restrictive, so a first declaration on the permissive pole takes a tool from assumed-restricted to declared-permissive with no change event to fire on at all. 86.0 percent of first declarations land permissive, holding between 83.4 and 94.0 percent under the omission of any single publisher. Under the schema conditional a re-approval trigger would have to fire on 445 tools where a watcher keyed to value flips reaches 62. That is a stronger argument for keeping annotations inside the digest scope than drift alone is, because a digest changes on a first declaration and a flip-watcher cannot fire on one. Paper, CC BY 4.0, Bharti and Agnihotri: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22649164\n\nThe figure I keep coming back to is a different one. 21,902 of your 23,821 changes have field-level comparisons, so 1,919 do not, lacking a digestible tool list on at least one side. That is 8.1 percent. On 4 September avp9-nexus argued for a three-state record that holds REJECT and UNMEASURABLE apart, on the grounds that a verifier should not report a failure it cannot distinguish from an absence. UNMEASURABLE now has a frequency, measured by someone who was not in that exchange. An 8.1 percent unmeasurable rate is an argument for the third state being normative rather than advisory.\n\nOn #18, I will take the corpus offer with the scope you gave it. Name, description, inputSchema and outputSchema re-canonicalized is item 1's scope minus annotations, which is enough to test whether a canonical form is stable across real declarations and not enough to test the annotation path. That is worth knowing before a field shape is fixed rather than after, and it is the half of the vector work in #29 and #30 that can proceed without waiting on the field definition.\n\nNot asking for a re-scope either. v0.2.0 is milestoned to March, and a record built from four independent sources over five months is a better thing to open that window with than a faster decision would have been.\n","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/GenAI-Security-Project/agent-control-standard/issues/comments/6465748971/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null}]